---
import Container from './Container.astro'
import logo from '../../../public/images/logo-ficho.svg?raw'
import LanguageSwitcher from './LanguageSwitcher.astro'
import { resolveLocale, useTranslations } from '../../i18n/ui'
import { getSite } from '../../content/site'
import { getRelativeLocaleUrl } from 'astro:i18n'

const lang = resolveLocale(Astro.currentLocale)
const site = getSite(lang)
const t = useTranslations(lang)
// getRelativeLocaleUrl('en', '/') returns "/en" (no trailing slash) while
// ('id', '/') returns "/" — normalize to always have exactly one trailing
// slash so fragment hrefs below never end up as "/en#work" or "/en//#work".
const homeHref = `${getRelativeLocaleUrl(lang, '/').replace(/\/$/, '')}/`

interface Props { showBackToHome?: boolean }
const { showBackToHome = false } = Astro.props
---
{/* Sticky, and it DOES have a bar — the two are one decision, which is why
    this comment has been rewritten twice. Figma's navbar (node 8921:2731's
    parent, 8920:2156) is a 1312x72 pill with a hairline border and a fill
    slightly lighter than the page. It was dropped along with the nav links
    when this header was reduced, and those were not the same decision: the
    links genuinely are absent from the design, the pill is not.

    On sticky: it was removed because "a sticky header without a background
    scrolls transparently over the content, which put 88px headings underneath
    the wordmark". Read the condition — *without a background*. That was true
    only while the pill was missing. With the pill back the header has an
    opaque surface of its own, content passing beneath it is covered, and the
    sole technical objection is gone. So sticky is restored deliberately, not
    reverted by accident, and the test that asserted the opposite was flipped
    on purpose.

    `sticky`, not `fixed`: sticky stays in flow, so nothing below shifts and no
    compensating padding has to be kept in sync with the header's height across
    breakpoints. `fixed` would buy nothing here and add exactly that coupling.

    `top-0`, not `top-4`, and the pt-8 does the offsetting instead. With
    `top-4` the header pins 16px down while its natural position is 0, so it
    sticks immediately and the pill sits 48px from the viewport top at rest —
    16px below where Figma draws it. `top-0` keeps the pill at the 32px pt-8
    gives it, both at rest and while pinned, so nothing moves on first scroll.

    The scrim is what makes that 32px gap safe, and its shape is deliberate.
    The pill is opaque, but the pt-8 above it belongs to this sticky element and
    was transparent, so content scrolled up through it — barely visible on the
    dark home page, glaring on the light case-study pages where large blue
    headings slid across the top edge.

    Two simpler fixes were tried in the browser and rejected on what they
    actually rendered. A flat opaque fill hid the content but covered
    body::before/::after for the header's full 104px, leaving a ~12-level
    brightness step at its bottom edge where the page's radial gradient resumed
    — the same class of seam the hero's fog mask exists to avoid. `backdrop-blur`
    alone left no seam but only smeared bright content, so a white work card
    still read as a pale smudge above the pill.

    This gradient does both: opaque through 40% (~42px) so the 0-32px gap is
    covered outright, then faded to nothing by 100%. The entire fade lives
    behind the opaque pill, so it is never seen, and alpha is already zero at
    the header's bottom edge — no step against the page. Do not flatten this to
    a solid colour.

    On the SectionIndicator: case-study pages fix a progress bar at
    `top: var(--header-h)`, which its own script sets from this header's
    measured height. That is 104px here (32 + 72) and it lands flush under the
    pill, so the two coordinate without either knowing the other's numbers.
    Changing pt-8 or the pill's height moves the indicator with it.

    Why the pill matters beyond fidelity: without it the header has no surface
    of its own and borrows whatever is behind it, so anything painted below —
    the hero's fog, for one — makes it read as a stray dark band. That is also
    what makes sticky safe now.

    Every value here is a token, not a hex. This header also renders on the
    light case-study pages, where BaseLayout remaps the colour tokens; a
    literal would have put a dark bar on #FAFAFA. Measured from Figma at full
    resolution the fill is #232324 -> #1A1B1D and the border is a gradient
    stroke #828287 -> #55555A. The gradient direction is reproduced through
    bg-elevated -> bg-secondary; the border is a flat token rather than a
    gradient ring, which is dimmer than Figma's stroke and the one deliberate
    approximation here. */}
<header
  class="sticky top-0 z-40 pt-8"
  style="background: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--color-bg-primary) 40%, transparent 100%)"
>
  <Container>
    {/* Logo pinned to the left; on home the section nav sits beside Contact Us
        on the right, on case study the "Back to Home" link takes its place. */}
    {/* Mobile: 3-column flex so the logo lands between the leading slot (empty
        on home, back link on case-study) and the trailing slot (burger on
        home, empty on case-study). Desktop switches to a 3-column grid so
        the wordmark sits dead-centre regardless of how wide the leading nav
        or trailing Contact button end up. */}
    <div class="flex h-[64px] items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-full stroke-gradient [--stroke-fill:linear-gradient(to_bottom,var(--color-bg-elevated),var(--color-bg-secondary))] pl-5 pr-2 md:grid md:h-[72px] md:grid-cols-3 md:pl-8 md:pr-3">
      {/* Slot 1 (leading): section nav on home, "Back to Home" on case study.
          On mobile home the nav is hidden AND this wrapper collapses out of
          the flex row (`hidden md:block`) so the logo becomes the leading
          flex child and `justify-between` pushes it to the pill's left edge.
          Case-study mobile keeps the wrapper visible so the back link renders.
          On desktop the wrapper is always `md:block` and the grid takes over. */}
      <div class:list={['md:justify-self-start', !showBackToHome && 'hidden md:block']}>
        {showBackToHome ? (
          <a
            href={homeHref}
            class="inline-flex h-8 items-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap text-base font-semibold text-text-primary transition hover:text-text-secondary"
          >
            <svg
              class="h-5 w-5"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <path d="M19 12H5" />
              <path d="m11 19-7-7 7-7" />
            </svg>
            <span>{t.header.backToHome}</span>
          </a>
        ) : (
          <nav aria-label="Sections" class="hidden md:flex items-center gap-6">
            {site.nav.map(item => (
              <a
                href={`${homeHref}${item.href}`}
                class="text-base font-medium text-text-secondary transition hover:text-text-primary"
              >{item.label}</a>
            ))}
          </nav>
        )}
      </div>

      {/* Slot 2: logo. Inlined rather than an <img> so its currentColor fill
          can inherit --color-text-primary — the light case-study pages remap
          that token, and a hardcoded white would vanish there. The accessible
          name lives on the link; the SVG is decoration.

          Hidden on mobile case study so the only element left in the pill is
          the leading "Back to Home" link. */}
      <a
        href={homeHref}
        class={`${showBackToHome ? 'hidden md:inline-flex' : 'inline-flex'} h-8 items-center text-text-primary [&>svg]:h-8 [&>svg]:w-auto md:justify-self-center`}
        aria-label={`${site.name} — home`}
      ><Fragment set:html={logo} /></a>

      {/* Slot 3 (trailing): Contact Me pill on md+, hamburger on mobile home.
          Wrapped in one div so the grid still has exactly three children. */}
      <div class="md:justify-self-end flex items-center gap-4">
        <div class="hidden md:flex">
          <LanguageSwitcher currentLang={lang} />
        </div>
        <a
          href={`mailto:${site.email}`}
          class="hidden h-12 items-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap rounded-full bg-text-primary px-5 text-base font-semibold leading-none text-bg-primary transition duration-300 hover:bg-text-secondary md:inline-flex"
        >
          <span>{t.header.contactMe}</span>
          <svg
            class="h-6 w-6"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M5 12h14" />
            <path d="m13 5 7 7-7 7" />
          </svg>
        </a>

        {/* Native <details> handles the open/close of the mobile menu without
            JS. list-none + the marker override strips the disclosure triangle
            so the summary reads as a plain icon button. Outside-click and
            in-menu link clicks are wired up in the script below. */}
        <details id="mobile-menu" class={`relative md:hidden ${showBackToHome ? 'hidden' : ''}`}>
        <summary
          aria-label={t.header.menu}
          class="inline-flex h-12 w-12 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-text-primary text-bg-primary transition duration-300 hover:bg-text-secondary [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
        >
          <svg
            class="h-6 w-6"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M4 6h16" />
            <path d="M4 12h16" />
            <path d="M4 18h16" />
          </svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="absolute right-0 top-[calc(100%+12px)] w-64 rounded-card stroke-gradient [--stroke-fill:var(--gradient-card)] p-5 shadow-card">
          <nav aria-label="Menu" class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            {site.nav.map(item => (
              <a
                href={`${homeHref}${item.href}`}
                class="text-base font-medium text-text-secondary transition hover:text-text-primary"
              >{item.label}</a>
            ))}
          </nav>
          <hr class="my-4 border-0 border-t border-border" />
          <LanguageSwitcher currentLang={lang} />
          <a
            href={`mailto:${site.email}`}
            class="inline-flex h-11 w-full items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-text-primary px-5 text-base font-semibold leading-none text-bg-primary transition duration-300 hover:bg-text-secondary"
          >
            <span>{t.header.contactMe}</span>
            <svg
              class="h-5 w-5"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <path d="M5 12h14" />
              <path d="m13 5 7 7-7 7" />
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
  <script>
    // Close the mobile burger menu when tapping outside it or activating any
    // link inside. <details> doesn't do either natively.
    const menu = document.getElementById('mobile-menu') as HTMLDetailsElement | null
    if (menu) {
      document.addEventListener('click', ev => {
        if (menu.open && !menu.contains(ev.target as Node)) menu.open = false
      })
      menu.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(a => {
        a.addEventListener('click', () => { menu.open = false })
      })
    }
  </script>
</header>
